definitions:
Fix an infinite set D of data values. An input schema is a finite set of relation names with specified finite arities. A database instance I is a finite set of facts \(R(a_{1},\ldots ,a_{r})\), with \(a_{i}\in D\). Its active domain \(adom(I)\) is the set of values occurring in those facts. J is an induced extension of I when \(adom(I)\subseteq adom(J)\) and, for every input relation R of arity r, Rᴵ=Rᴶ∩\(adom(I)\)ʳ. Thus all relations on old tuples are unchanged; every new fact of positive arity contains at least one new value. Nullary facts must remain unchanged. A query q of arity k maps I to a finite set of k-tuples, and is generic if permuting D commutes with q. For \(k=0\), the empty tuple represents true. Membership in E means \(q(I)\subseteq q(J)\) for every such pair I,J. A program has finitely many rules H(ȳ)←\(A_{1},\ldots ,A_{u},
