e w_{s}\). Atoms use relation names and variables, with no constants, function symbols or value invention. Every variable in the head, a negative atom or a disequality must also occur in a positive body atom. Input relations are extensional and never defined by a rule; derived relations are intensional. A stratification assigns levels to intensional relations so that positive dependencies have no higher level than the head and negative dependencies have strictly lower level. Evaluate levels in order, starting new derived relations empty and repeatedly adding every head whose instantiated body holds until no further fact is added; lower levels and input facts stay fixed. The distinguished output relation defines q. A semi-positive program permits negative atoms only on input relations. Equality can be represented by repeated variables, and disequality is actual inequality of data values. Programs may introduce finitely many auxiliary intensional relations of arbitrary finite arity. The requested equivalence is equality of outputs on every finite input instance, with no built-in order or additional input relations.
